The '''2011 UEFA Super Cup''' was the 36th [[UEFA Super Cup]], between the reigning champions of the two club competitions organised by the [[Europe]]an [[association football|football]] governing body [[UEFA]]: the [[UEFA Champions League]] and the [[UEFA Europa League]]. It took place at the [[Stade Louis II]] in [[Monaco]] on 26 August 2011.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.dfb.de/index.php?id=510208 |title=Rahmenterminkalender 2011/2012|work=DFB.de |publisher=Deutscher Fussball-Bund |date=24 November 2010 |access-date=17 April 2011|language=de }}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.lfp.fr/calendriers/2011_2012/calendrier_2011_2012.pdf |title=Calendrier Général des Compétitions 2011/2012|work=LFP.fr |publisher=Ligue de Football Professionnel |date=31 March 2011 |access-date=17 April 2011|language=fr | archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20110525095446/http://www.lfp.fr/calendriers/2011_2012/calendrier_2011_2012.pdf| archive-date= 25 May 2011 | url-status= live}}</ref> It was contested by the [[2010–11 UEFA Champions League]] winners [[FC Barcelona|Barcelona]] of [[Royal Spanish Football Federation|Spain]] and the [[2010–11 UEFA Europa League]] winners [[FC Porto|Porto]] of [[Portuguese Football Federation|Portugal]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefasupercup/news/0250-0c50f41ca58d-1aecbba02039-1000--barcelona-and-porto-to-contest-super-cup/|title=Barcelona and Porto to contest Super Cup|publisher=[[UEFA]]|date=29 May 2011}}</ref> Barcelona won the title defeating Porto 2–0.<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://soccernet.espn.go.com/report/_/id/331104?cc=5739|title=Cesc seals Super Cup win for Barca|date=26 August 2011|work=ESPN Soccernet|access-date=30 August 2011|archive-date=2 November 2012|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121102061807/http://soccernet.espn.go.com/report/_/id/331104?cc=5739|url-status=dead}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2011/aug/26/cesc-fabregas-barcelona-porto|title=Cesc Fábregas scores first Barcelona goal in Uefa Super Cup triumph|date=26 August 2011|work=The Guardian|access-date=30 August 2011}}</ref>

==Venue==
The [[Stade Louis II]] in [[Monaco]] has been the venue for the UEFA Super Cup every year since 1998. Built in 1985, the stadium is also the home of [[AS Monaco FC|AS Monaco]], who play in the French league system.

